deep.reconstruction:
This page is the back or front of an institutional envelope from the Institut de Mathématiques, USTL Montpellier, addressed by hand to 'M. Grothendieck'. Grothendieck reused it as scrap paper. The only mathematical content is two rough pen sketches, with no text, formulas or labels.

(i) Upper right: an almost closed loop, open on one side, with a single dot inside. It reads as a disc or neighbourhood around a marked point or puncture.

(ii) Lower: a large circle containing three things:
- a smaller circle with a dot inside it (plus a tiny mark);
- two very small circled dots.

The most natural reading, given the folder theme ('Chirurgie des surfaces conformes') and the neighbouring pages, is a schematic Riemann surface or disc with marked points. Each puncture is surrounded by a small disc or collar neighbourhood, and one puncture has a larger annular neighbourhood. That annulus is the kind of region D* ≅ {0<|q|<r} that the neighbouring pages parametrise, and that appears as C*/b^Z (with 0<|b|<1) in the Tate-curve-like description on the next page.

The neighbouring pages tabulate equivalent data:
- (elliptic curve X, oriented closed geodesic Γ);
- (X, a cusp or a horodisc chart in H);
- (X, a ∈ π1(X));
- a discrete subgroup Z ≅ Z of C*, generated by b with 0<|b|<1;
- a complex line E with a torsor under C*.

The sketch plausibly illustrates the surgery step these data serve. In that step a small disc around a puncture is excised and a standard annulus is glued along the collar (plumbing / grafting), or a cusp is opened into a closed geodesic. This reading cannot be verified from the page itself. No statement, construction or result is written here.
